WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.600 --> 00:00:14.082
Hi! A tall tree has no heart, yet water from its roots reaches the highest leaves, and food from the leaves reaches the roots. How? Let's learn about the plant transport system.

2
00:00:14.232 --> 00:00:18.906
Inside a plant's stem, there are two kinds of very thin tubes.

3
00:00:19.056 --> 00:00:28.530
The first kind are the water-carrying tubes. They carry water and dissolved mineral salts from the roots, up the stem, to the leaves and other parts.

4
00:00:28.680 --> 00:00:40.242
The second kind are the food-carrying tubes. They carry the food made in the leaves to every part of the plant, including the roots. They are near the outside of the stem, just under the bark.

5
00:00:40.392 --> 00:00:46.434
So water travels upwards, while food goes to all parts of the plant.

6
00:00:46.584 --> 00:00:51.810
Let's try an experiment: put a stick of celery into water coloured red.

7
00:00:51.960 --> 00:00:55.746
After a few hours, the celery leaves turn red.

8
00:00:55.896 --> 00:01:02.106
Cut across the stem, and you'll see little red dots. Those are the water-carrying tubes.

9
00:01:02.256 --> 00:01:08.250
This shows that water travels upwards through the water-carrying tubes.

10
00:01:08.400 --> 00:01:16.818
What happens if we remove a ring of bark from a tree trunk? Removing the bark also removes the food-carrying tubes just under it.

11
00:01:16.968 --> 00:01:24.882
Food made in the leaves travels down but cannot get past the ring. It builds up there, so the part just above the ring swells.

12
00:01:25.032 --> 00:01:32.346
The parts below the ring and the roots get no food. After a long time, the roots die, and so does the tree.

13
00:01:32.496 --> 00:01:40.242
But at first the leaves still get water, because the water-carrying tubes are deeper inside and were not removed.

14
00:01:40.392 --> 00:01:48.858
Humans have a transport system too: the circulatory system. The heart pumps blood through blood vessels around the body.

15
00:01:49.008 --> 00:01:55.290
What's the same: both carry water, food and other substances to all parts of the body.

16
00:01:55.440 --> 00:02:03.378
What's different: plants have no heart and no blood. They use two kinds of tubes instead.

17
00:02:03.528 --> 00:02:11.250
Watch out! Some pupils say the water-carrying tubes carry water down to the roots. That's back to front!

18
00:02:11.400 --> 00:02:18.066
The roots take in water from the soil, and the water-carrying tubes carry it upwards to the stem and leaves.

19
00:02:18.216 --> 00:02:23.778
What goes down to the roots is food, in the food-carrying tubes.

20
00:02:23.928 --> 00:02:33.138
Your turn! The food-carrying tubes carry food from where to where? Pause and think.

21
00:02:33.288 --> 00:02:40.002
From the leaves to all parts of the plant, including the roots. Did you get it right?

22
00:02:40.152 --> 00:02:50.586
Another one! After a ring of bark is removed, why does the part just above it swell? Pause and think.

23
00:02:50.736 --> 00:03:00.810
Because the food-carrying tubes were removed. Food from the leaves can't pass down, so it builds up above the ring. Did you get it right?

24
00:03:00.960 --> 00:03:11.322
Last one! Celery is put in red water. Which kind of tubes turn red? Pause and think.

25
00:03:11.472 --> 00:03:19.026
The water-carrying tubes. The red water travels up through them. Did you get it right?

26
00:03:19.176 --> 00:03:34.962
Remember: water-carrying tubes take water and mineral salts up from the roots. Food-carrying tubes, just under the bark, take food from the leaves to all parts. Humans use the circulatory system; plants use these two kinds of tubes.

27
00:03:35.112 --> 00:03:39.306
Try the celery experiment at home!

